  "summary": "Respiratory and influenza studies now model viral dispersal across spatial scales, connecting spread within tissue to interferon responses.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is spatially explicit treatment of viral infection, in which dispersal shapes host response. A respiratory preprint separates within-host spread into local expansion and occasional long-range dissemination that seeds new infection foci, explicitly bridging spatial scales. A published spatially explicit stochastic model of in vitro influenza A infection integrates defective interfering particles, IFN signalling and dispersal to shape the type I interferon response. Across these items, interferon readouts appear in the published model, while explicit scale bridging appears in the preprint. A live attenuated influenza vaccine built around autophagy targeting and a bat alphacoronavirus study showing CEACAM6-dependent cell entry are adjacent context and do not model spatial dynamics.",
